// Case bank: every syllabus case, Arts. 2–11 (midterm coverage).
// Fields: id, n (title), c (cite), t (topic key), g (subgroup), h (1–3 word hook), hc (g=liable affirmed, r=acquitted/not liable, y=modified, b=principle),
// f (facts), r (ruling), w (why: element met or failed), d (one-line doctrine), k (recit nitpicks), x (flag / source conflict)
window.CASES = [
// ───────── ART. 2 ─────────
{id:"bull",n:"U.S. v. Bull",c:"15 Phil. 7 (1910)",t:"a2",g:"Foreign merchant vessels",h:"Cattle in transit",hc:"g",
f:"H. N. Bull, master of the <b>Norwegian</b> steamship <i>Standard</i>, carried <b>cattle and carabaos</b> from Formosa to Manila without suitable means of securing them (the animals were tied by ropes through their noses, so many were injured), in violation of <b>Act No. 55</b> as amended by Act No. 275. Defense: the act happened on the high seas, on a foreign ship, so Philippine courts had no jurisdiction.",
r:"<b>Conviction affirmed; Philippine courts had jurisdiction.</b>",
w:"The forbidden condition <b>continued</b> while the ship was inside Philippine territorial waters (within 3 miles of the headlands at the entrance to Manila Bay), so the offense was committed here too. A foreign <b>merchant</b> vessel in our waters is subject to our laws (English rule); its flag does not matter.",
d:"A continuing offense on a foreign merchant vessel is triable here once the vessel is within Philippine waters while the offense continues.",
k:["Norwegian ship, not English","Animal-cruelty statute (Act No. 55), not the RPC","Reyes uses Bull to explain the French vs. English rules; the Philippines follows the English rule"]},

{id:"vanwilsem",n:"Del Socorro v. Van Wilsem",c:"G.R. No. 194707, 10 Dec. 2014",t:"a2",g:"Generality / territoriality",h:"Dutch dad, no support",hc:"y",
f:"Norma Del Socorro, a Filipina, married Ernst Johan Brinkman <b>Van Wilsem</b>, a <b>Dutch</b> national, in Holland; they had a son. After their <b>divorce in the Netherlands</b>, she and the child moved to the Philippines. He promised monthly support but never gave any. He later moved to <b>Cebu</b> and remarried. She sued under <b>RA 9262 § 5(e)</b> (deprivation of financial support). The RTC dismissed the case: as a foreigner, he was not bound by Philippine law on support.",
r:"<b>Petition granted; case remanded for trial.</b> A foreigner residing here can be liable under RA 9262 for refusing to support his child.",
w:"(1) Under Art. 15 of the Civil Code, a foreigner's duty to support is governed by his national law, but he <b>never proved Dutch law</b>, so under <b>processual presumption</b> it is presumed to be the same as ours, and he must support his child. (2) Under the <b>principle of generality / territoriality</b> (Civil Code Art. 14), penal laws bind all who live or sojourn in the Philippines. His denial of support is a <b>continuing</b> act committed here, where he lives.",
d:"Penal laws bind foreigners who live or sojourn in the Philippines; unproved foreign law is presumed to be the same as ours (processual presumption).",
k:["The divorce happened abroad, but the refusal to give support continued in the Philippines","Generality + territoriality in one case"]},

{id:"wongcheng",n:"People v. Wong Cheng",c:"46 Phil. 729 (1922)",t:"a2",g:"Foreign merchant vessels",h:"Opium smoked aboard",hc:"y",
f:"Wong Cheng <b>smoked opium</b> aboard the <b>English</b> merchant vessel <i>Changsa</i> while it was <b>anchored 2½ miles from the shores of Manila</b>. The trial court <b>dismissed</b> the case for lack of jurisdiction.",
r:"<b>Dismissal reversed; case remanded.</b> Philippine courts have jurisdiction.",
w:"Under the English rule, crimes aboard a foreign merchant ship in our waters are triable here unless they affect only the internal management of the ship. Smoking opium here makes the drug produce its harmful effects <b>within our territory</b>, so it is a <b>breach of public order</b>.",
d:"Smoking opium on a foreign merchant vessel anchored in Philippine waters is a breach of public order, so it is triable here.",
k:["Anchored 2½ miles off Manila","Ship's name: Changsa (English)","Compare Look Chaw: mere possession while in transit is not triable"]},

{id:"lookchaw",n:"U.S. v. Look Chaw (syllabus: Look Chow)",c:"18 Phil. 573 (1910)",t:"a2",g:"Foreign merchant vessels",h:"Opium landed, sold",hc:"g",
f:"Look Chaw had opium aboard the <b>English</b> steamship <i>Erroll</i>, which was <b>in transit</b> from Hong Kong to Mexico and anchored at Cebu. Revenue agents found sacks of opium aboard. He had also <b>sold</b> part of the opium here.",
r:"<b>Convicted; Philippine courts had jurisdiction</b> (for the opium brought out and sold here).",
w:"<b>Mere possession</b> of opium aboard a foreign vessel in transit is not triable here, because it causes none of the effects our law seeks to prevent. But once the opium is <b>landed</b> on Philippine soil or used here, that is an open violation of our laws.",
d:"Mere possession aboard a foreign vessel in transit is not triable here; landing or using the drug here is.",
k:["In transit: Hong Kong → Mexico, via Cebu","The difference from Ah Sing: Ah Sing's ship ended its voyage here"]},

{id:"ahsing",n:"U.S. v. Ah Sing",c:"36 Phil. 978 (1917)",t:"a2",g:"Foreign merchant vessels",h:"Opium, terminal port",hc:"g",
f:"Ah Sing, a Chinese crewman (fireman) of the foreign steamship <i>Shun Chang</i>, bought opium in <b>Saigon</b> and kept it aboard. The ship arrived in <b>Cebu</b>, its <b>port of destination</b>, where the opium was found. He was charged with <b>illegal importation</b> and argued he had no intent to import it.",
r:"<b>Conviction for illegal importation affirmed.</b>",
w:"Since the Philippines was the vessel's <b>terminal port</b> (not in transit), the importation was <b>consummated</b> when the ship entered port with the drug aboard. Landing it or passing it through customs is not required, and intent to import is presumed from bringing it in.",
d:"Bringing prohibited drugs into a Philippine port that is the vessel's destination consummates illegal importation.",
k:["Saigon → Cebu","Ship: Shun Chang","Not in transit, which distinguishes it from Look Chaw"]},

{id:"lollo",n:"People v. Lol-lo and Saraw",c:"43 Phil. 19 (1922)",t:"a2",g:"Crimes against the law of nations",h:"Moro pirates",hc:"g",
f:"In the <b>Dutch East Indies</b>, armed Moros in vintas, including Lol-lo and Saraw, attacked a boat of <b>Dutch subjects</b>: they took the cargo, wounded some men, <b>raped</b> two women (Lol-lo raped one), and cut holes in the boat to sink it. The women later escaped. Lol-lo and Saraw returned to <b>Tawi-Tawi, Sulu</b>, where they were arrested and charged with <b>piracy</b>.",
r:"<b>Piracy conviction affirmed.</b> Philippine courts had jurisdiction.",
w:"Piracy is a crime against all mankind; pirates are <b><i>hostes humani generis</i></b> (enemies of mankind), so piracy may be tried wherever the offender is found. The jurisdiction of nations over piracy is not limited by territory. This is the basis for Art. 2(5) (crimes against the law of nations).",
d:"Piracy is triable anywhere, since pirates are enemies of all mankind.",
k:["High seas near the Dutch East Indies; the victims were Dutch","Arrested in Tawi-Tawi, Sulu","Links to Art. 2(5): Title One of Book Two (piracy is in Art. 122)"]},

// ───────── ART. 3 ─────────
{id:"ahchong",n:"U.S. v. Ah Chong",c:"15 Phil. 488 (1910)",t:"a3",g:"Mistake of fact",h:"Roommate 'robber'",hc:"r",
f:"Ah Chong, a <b>cook</b> at officers' quarters in <b>Fort McKinley</b>, shared a room with <b>Pascual Gualberto</b>, a houseboy. There had been robberies nearby, and the door had no lock, so Ah Chong braced it with a <b>chair</b>. At about 10:00 p.m., he was awakened by someone trying to force the door. He called out twice, “<i>Quién es?</i>” (“Who is there?”), but got no answer. He warned, “If you enter the room I will kill you.” The intruder pushed the door open and Ah Chong was <b>struck by the chair</b>. In the dark, believing he was being attacked by a robber, he grabbed the <b>kitchen knife he kept under his pillow</b> and stabbed the intruder. It was Pascual, his roommate, who died the next day.",
r:"<b>Acquitted — mistake of fact.</b> (The trial court had convicted him of homicide.)",
w:"Had the facts been as he believed, the act would have been <b>lawful self-defense</b>: an intruder forcing his way in at night is unlawful aggression, the knife was a reasonable means, and he gave no provocation. He acted in <b>good faith and without negligence</b>: in the dark and under sudden attack, he had no time to verify. With no criminal intent, there is no felony: <i>actus non facit reum nisi mens sit rea</i>.",
d:"An honest, reasonable mistake of fact, made without fault or carelessness, negates criminal intent.",
k:["Block intel: read Ah Chong FIVE times; Sir asks tiny details","The chair braced against the unlocked door","He shouted 'Who is there?' twice, then 'If you enter the room I will kill you'","The knife was kept under his pillow","The victim was his roommate, the houseboy Pascual Gualberto"],
x:"Sir Barry is nitpicky on this case. Re-read the original for exact names, times, and the knife's location before the exam."},

{id:"oanis",n:"People v. Oanis",c:"74 Phil. 257 (1943)",t:"a3",g:"Mistake of fact",h:"Shot the sleeper",hc:"y",
f:"Chief of Police <b>Antonio Oanis</b> and Corporal <b>Alberto Galanta</b> were ordered to arrest <b>Anselmo Balagtas</b>, a notorious criminal and escaped convict, and to get him <b>“dead or alive”</b> if he resisted. They were told he was in the house of his <i>querida</i>, the bailarina <b>Irene</b>, in Cabanatuan. They went into her room, saw a man <b>sleeping with his back to the door</b>, and both fired at him at once without asking who he was. He was <b>Serapio Tecson</b>, an innocent man (Irene's paramour). The trial court convicted them of homicide through reckless imprudence.",
r:"<b>Guilty of murder</b> (not reckless imprudence), with the privileged mitigating circumstance of <b>incomplete fulfillment of duty</b> (Art. 69).",
w:"There was no innocent mistake of fact. Unlike Ah Chong, nothing pressed them to act at once: the man was asleep, so they had <b>time to verify his identity</b> and make a bloodless arrest. Even if he had been Balagtas, killing him in his sleep was unjustified, because force is allowed only if the fugitive resists. Shooting deliberately is <b>dolo</b>, not <i>culpa</i>, so the crime cannot be reckless imprudence. They were on duty (first requisite of Art. 11(5)), but the killing was not the <b>necessary consequence</b> of that duty, so the justification was incomplete.",
d:"A mistake made through fault or carelessness does not exempt; officers who kill without verifying identity, when they had time to, are liable (at most mitigated by incomplete fulfillment of duty).",
k:["Ah Chong vs. Oanis: no time to verify (Ah Chong) vs. time to verify (Oanis)","Ordered to get Balagtas 'dead or alive' only if he resisted","Also cited under Art. 11(5), incomplete fulfillment of duty"],
x:"Some summaries call the crime “homicide with reckless imprudence.” That was the trial court's ruling; the Supreme Court found murder (Reyes, p. 46)."},

{id:"magsumbol",n:"Magsumbol v. People",c:"G.R. No. 207175, 26 Nov. 2014",t:"a3",g:"Mistake of fact / intent to gain",h:"Coconut trees, unclear boundary",hc:"r",
f:"The accused cut down <b>coconut trees</b> and made them into lumber, believing the trees stood on the land of the person who authorized them (an Avanzado relative). The trees turned out to be on <b>adjacent land</b> (Menandro Avanzado's), and the boundary between the two lots was <b>not clearly marked</b>. They were charged with <b>theft</b>.",
r:"<b>Acquitted.</b>",
w:"Theft requires <b>intent to gain</b>. Because the boundaries were uncertain and they cut the trees with the permission of someone they believed owned the land, they acted in <b>good faith</b>. If the facts had been as they believed, the cutting would have been lawful. Their honest mistake of fact negated criminal intent.",
d:"A good-faith belief that one is authorized to take property negates intent to gain in theft.",
k:["Uncertain boundary between adjoining lots","Charged with theft, not malicious mischief"],
x:"Reviewers disagree on who authorized the cutting. Check the original if Sir asks for names."},

{id:"gervero",n:"People v. Gervero",c:"G.R. No. 206725, 11 July 2018",t:"a3",g:"Mistake of fact",h:"'Thought they were NPA'",hc:"g",
f:"The accused, who were on <b>tactical patrol against the NPA</b>, shot and killed the victims in a rice field hours after an earlier encounter at one victim's house. Charged with murder, they claimed <b>mistake of fact</b>: they did not recognize the victims and thought they were NPA rebels.",
r:"<b>Conviction for murder affirmed.</b> Mistake of fact rejected.",
w:"The mistake was not reasonable: the area was open and lighted, they had <b>met the victims only hours earlier</b>, and the victims identified themselves. Even if the victims had been NPA members, killing them this way would still have been unlawful, because the act would not have been lawful under the facts as the accused believed them.",
d:"For mistake of fact to exempt, the belief must be reasonable and the act must have been lawful under the facts as believed; neither was true here.",
k:["The two-question test: (1) Was the belief reasonable? (2) If true, would the act be lawful?"]},

// ───────── ART. 4 ¶1 ─────────
{id:"iligan",n:"People v. Iligan",c:"191 SCRA 643, G.R. No. 75369 (1990)",t:"a4a",g:"Proximate cause",h:"Hacked, then run over",hc:"y",
f:"After an altercation, <b>Fernando Iligan</b> hacked <b>Esmeraldo Quiñones, Jr.</b> on the head with a <b>bolo</b>. The victim was later <b>run over by a vehicle</b>. The death certificate gave the cause of death as shock and massive cerebral hemorrhages <b>due to a vehicular accident</b>, and the physician who did the autopsy called the hacking wound superficial.",
r:"<b>Iligan liable for the death — homicide</b> (not murder). Co-accused Edmundo Asis was acquitted.",
w:"The hacking may not have been the immediate cause of death, but it was the <b>proximate cause</b>: it started an unbroken chain of events that ended in the vehicle running over the victim. <i>El que es causa de la causa es causa del mal causado.</i> That the wound was superficial does not matter. Treachery was not proven, since a sudden attack is not treachery by itself, so the crime is homicide. Asis's <b>mere presence</b> did not make him a conspirator.",
d:"One who sets off a chain of events that leads to death is liable for the death, even if another force (here, a vehicle) delivered the fatal injury.",
k:["The death certificate said 'vehicular accident'","The autopsy called the head wound 'superficial'","Also cited: mere presence ≠ conspiracy; suddenness ≠ treachery"]},

{id:"mananquil",n:"People v. Mananquil",c:"132 SCRA 196, G.R. No. L-35574 (1984)",t:"a4a",g:"Proximate cause",h:"Gasoline on husband",hc:"y",
f:"On 6 March 1965, at the NAWASA compound in Pasay, <b>Valentina Mananquil</b> poured <b>gasoline</b> on her husband, <b>Elias Day</b>, and set him on fire. He was brought to the hospital and died on <b>10 March 1965</b>, four days later. She was charged with <b>parricide</b>. Her defense: he was drunk and cursing her, she felt dizzy, and she did not realize she was splashing him with gasoline.",
r:"<b>Parricide affirmed.</b>",
w:"The issue is whether the burns, or an independent cause, were the <b>proximate cause</b> of death. Whichever way the Court ruled, the test is the same: was the death the <b>direct, natural, and logical consequence</b> of the felonious act, with no efficient intervening cause?",
d:"Art. 4(1): an offender is liable for the death only if the felonious act is its proximate cause; an efficient intervening cause breaks the chain.",
k:["Gasoline + match","Four days between the burning and the death"]},

{id:"brobst",n:"U.S. v. Brobst",c:"14 Phil. 310 (1909)",t:"a4a",g:"Proximate cause",h:"One fist blow",hc:"g",
f:"Brobst, angry, struck the victim a <b>hard blow with his fist</b> (below the ribs). The victim fell, got up, walked away, and <b>died shortly afterward</b>. No autopsy was done. The defense argued that the cause of death was not proven and that he did not intend to kill.",
r:"<b>Guilty of homicide.</b>",
w:"The victim was apparently healthy before the blow and died soon after it, with no other explanation. The blow was the <b>proximate cause</b>, and death was its natural consequence. One is not relieved of liability for the natural consequences of an illegal act merely because one did not intend them (Reyes cites Brobst for exactly this).",
d:"One is liable for the natural consequences of one's felonious act even if they were not intended.",
k:["No autopsy, but the circumstances (a healthy victim who died right after the blow) proved causation"],
x:"The reviewers give slightly different facts (one says the victim reached his sister's house before dying). Stick to the doctrine unless you have read the original."},

{id:"garcia",n:"Garcia v. People",c:"597 SCRA 392, G.R. No. 171951 (2009)",t:"a4a",g:"Proximate cause / praeter intentionem",h:"Mauled heart patient",hc:"y",
f:"After an argument (the accused's drinking had caused disturbances), the accused mauled the victim, <b>Manuel Chy</b>, who had a <b>heart condition</b>. The physical injuries themselves were not serious, but the victim died of a <b>heart attack</b> soon after the mauling.",
r:"<b>Homicide</b>, with the mitigating circumstance of <b>no intention to commit so grave a wrong</b> (Art. 13(3), praeter intentionem).",
w:"The mauling was the <b>proximate cause</b>: the excitement and the blows triggered the fatal heart attack in a man whose heart was already weak. A pre-existing illness of the victim is <b>not an efficient intervening cause</b>. He who commits a felony takes his victim as he finds him.",
d:"The victim's pre-existing illness does not break the causal chain; the assailant is liable for the death, at most mitigated by praeter intentionem.",
k:["Heart attack, not the bruises, was the immediate cause","Art. 4(1) plus Art. 13(3) in one case: a favorite combination on exams"]},

// ───────── ART. 4 ¶2 ─────────
{id:"balmores",n:"People v. Balmores",c:"85 Phil. 493 (1950)",t:"a4b",g:"Impossible crime",h:"Doctored sweepstakes ticket",hc:"g",
f:"On 22 Sept. 1947, Rafael Balmores tore off part of a genuine <b>⅛ unit Philippine Charity Sweepstakes ticket</b> and wrote in ink the winning number <b>074000</b> (from the 29 June 1947 draw). He tried to cash it for the prize of <b>₱1,359.55</b>. Bayani Miller, the employee, saw that it was falsified and called a policeman. Balmores pleaded guilty without counsel to <b>attempted estafa through falsification</b>, then argued on appeal that the facts constituted no offense, or at most an impossible crime.",
r:"<b>Conviction affirmed: attempted estafa through falsification</b> of a security. No impossible crime.",
w:"The crime was not inherently impossible, and the means were not inadequate or ineffectual. It failed only because the employee was <b>alert enough to discover</b> the forgery, which is a cause independent of the offender's will (an attempt). Clumsiness or a crude forgery does not make a crime impossible.",
d:"A crime that fails only because the victim was alert, not because it was inherently impossible, is an attempted felony, not an impossible crime.",
k:["Bayani Miller spotted the forgery","The 29 June 1947 draw","Pleaded guilty without counsel"]},

{id:"intod",n:"Intod v. Court of Appeals",c:"215 SCRA 52 (1992)",t:"a4b",g:"Impossible crime",h:"Empty bedroom",hc:"y",
f:"<b>Sulpicio Intod</b>, Jorge Pangasian, Santos Tubio, and Avelino Daligdig were recruited by <b>Aniceto Dumalagan</b>, who wanted <b>Bernardina Palangpangan</b> killed over a <b>land dispute</b>; Salvador Mandaya was made to go along. At about <b>10:00 p.m. on 4 Feb. 1979</b> in Katugasan, Lopez Jaena, Misamis Occidental, Mandaya pointed out her bedroom and the four <b>fired into it</b>. Bernardina was <b>in another city</b> that night; her son-in-law's family was in the house, and no one was hit. The RTC and CA convicted them of <b>attempted murder</b>.",
r:"<b>Modified: impossible crime</b> (Art. 4(2)), punished under Art. 59 (arresto mayor).",
w:"Murder was <b>factually (physically) impossible</b> because the victim was not in the room. Factual impossibility is a form of <b>inherent impossibility</b> under Art. 4(2), so the Court did not follow the American rule that treats such cases as attempts. Intent to kill and an act against persons were both present, and the act violated no other provision of the RPC, so all the requisites of an impossible crime were met.",
d:"Factual impossibility (the victim is absent) is inherent impossibility; shooting into an empty room with intent to kill is an impossible crime.",
k:["10:00 p.m., land dispute","Mandaya pointed out the room","The intended victim was in another city"]},

{id:"domasian",n:"People v. Domasian",c:"219 SCRA 245 (1993)",t:"a4b",g:"Impossible crime",h:"Ransom note too late",hc:"g",
f:"On the morning of <b>11 March 1982</b> in Lopez, Quezon, <b>8-year-old Enrico Paulo Agra</b> was walking with a classmate when <b>Pablito Domasian</b> asked his help in getting a medical certificate signed. Domasian took the boy by tricycle to Calantipayan, then forced him onto a <b>minibus</b> to Gumaca and on to San Vicente, holding him firmly. Tricycle driver <b>Alexander Grate</b> grew suspicious (Domasian claimed they were brothers despite their obvious differences) and reported it to barangay tanods; Domasian fled and Enrico was rescued. That afternoon, Enrico's father, Dr. Agra, received a <b>ransom note</b> later found to be written by co-accused <b>Dr. Samson Tan</b>. Tan argued that the ransom note was at most an <b>impossible crime</b>, since the boy was already free when it arrived.",
r:"<b>Kidnapping and serious illegal detention; conviction of both affirmed.</b>",
w:"No impossible crime: (1) kidnapping is a crime against <b>liberty</b>, not against persons or property; and (2) the crime was <b>already consummated</b> the moment the boy was deprived of his liberty. The ransom note's late arrival only affects the penalty; it does not make the crime impossible.",
d:"Impossible crime covers only acts that would be offenses against persons or property; a consummated kidnapping cannot become impossible because the ransom note came late.",
k:["Enrico was 8; the father was a doctor","Grate the tricycle driver was the hero","Domasian claimed they were 'brothers'","The ransom note was written by a different accused (Dr. Tan)"]},

{id:"jacinto",n:"Jacinto v. People",c:"G.R. No. 162540, 13 July 2009",t:"a4b",g:"Impossible crime",h:"Bounced stolen check",hc:"y",
f:"In June 1997, customer Isabelita Aquino Milabo (“Baby Aquino”) gave <b>Gemma Jacinto</b>, a collector of <b>Mega Foam</b>, a <b>₱10,000 BDO check</b> payable to Mega Foam. Instead of remitting it, Jacinto had it deposited in the Land Bank account of Generoso Capitle, husband of her sister Jacqueline. The check <b>bounced</b> (insufficient funds). Employee Rowena Ricablanca learned of it and reported it; with the NBI, the owner (Joseph Dyhengco) set up an <b>entrapment</b> using marked money as a 'replacement' for the check. Jacinto, Anita Valencia, and Jacqueline Capitle were charged with qualified theft.",
r:"<b>Modified: impossible crime</b> (qualified theft could not be produced).",
w:"Theft requires taking personal property <b>with value</b> and with intent to gain. The check was <b>worthless</b> from the start (unfunded), so there was nothing of value to gain. That is <b>legal (factual) impossibility</b>. The intent to steal and the taking were present, so Art. 4(2) applied. The marked money from the entrapment was not taken with intent to gain from Mega Foam, since it was planted.",
d:"Stealing a check that turns out to be worthless is an impossible crime of theft: the inherent impossibility lies in the absence of anything of value.",
k:["Block note: Sir is VERY particular with Jacinto's names; skim them before the exam","Rowena Ricablanca was the 'snitch'","₱10,000 BDO check from Baby Aquino","Deposited in the Land Bank account of the sister's husband"]},

// ───────── ART. 6 ─────────
{id:"eduave",n:"U.S. v. Eduave",c:"36 Phil. 209 (1917)",t:"a6",g:"Murder / homicide",h:"Bolo, left for dead",hc:"g",
f:"Protasio Eduave, the girl's <i>querido</i> who lived with her family, was angry because she had accused him of <b>raping</b> her and getting her pregnant. He rushed at her and struck her <b>from behind</b> with a sharp <b>bolo</b>, leaving a gash in the lumbar region and side <b>8½ inches long and 2 inches deep</b>. Believing she was dead, he <b>threw her body into the bushes</b>, then gave himself up and declared he had killed her. She survived.",
r:"<b>Frustrated murder</b> (treachery: a sudden attack from behind).",
w:"He performed <b>all the acts of execution</b> needed to kill, and believed he had killed her (the <b>subjective phase was complete</b>). Death did not result for reasons independent of his will. That is <b>frustration</b>, not attempt: in an attempt the offender does not complete all the acts of execution.",
d:"When the offender performs every act that should produce death and believes the victim dead, but death does not result for reasons independent of his will, the felony is frustrated.",
k:["8½ in. long, 2 in. deep","He threw her 'body' into the bushes and surrendered","The classic distinction between attempted and frustrated (subjective/objective phases)"],
x:"Some summaries say he “voluntarily desisted.” That is wrong: he stopped because he believed she was already dead. Desistance applies only to attempts."},

{id:"orita",n:"People v. Orita",c:"184 SCRA 105 (1990)",t:"a6",g:"Rape",h:"No frustrated rape",hc:"y",
f:"Early on <b>20 March 1983</b>, <b>Cristina Abayan</b>, a 19-year-old freshman at <b>St. Joseph's College</b>, Borongan, Eastern Samar, came home from a party. At the door of her boarding house, <b>Ceilito Orita</b>, a Philippine Constabulary soldier, held a <b>balisong</b> to her neck and forced her upstairs. He abused her; when he lay down and ordered her to mount him, he could not achieve <b>full penetration</b>. She escaped, <b>jumped out a window</b>, and ran <b>naked</b> to the <b>municipal building</b> about 18 m away. The RTC convicted him of <b>frustrated rape</b>.",
r:"<b>Modified: consummated rape</b>; reclusion perpetua.",
w:"Rape is consummated by <b>carnal knowledge</b>, and <b>any penetration</b> of the female organ, even slight, is enough; full penetration is not required. Once carnal knowledge occurs, every element is present, and before it, not all the acts of execution have been performed. So there is <b>no frustrated rape</b>; there is only attempted or consummated rape.",
d:"There is no frustrated rape: the slightest penetration consummates the crime.",
k:["Balisong knife","She jumped out the window and ran naked to the municipal building","Orita was a PC soldier","The RTC said frustrated; the Supreme Court said consummated"]},

{id:"campuhan",n:"People v. Campuhan",c:"329 SCRA 270 (2000, en banc)",t:"a6",g:"Rape",h:"Kneeling, pants down",hc:"y",
f:"At about 4:00 p.m. on 25 April 1996, <b>Corazon Pamintuan</b> went downstairs to make <b>Milo</b> for her children. <b>Primo Campuhan</b>, her brother's helper, was there filling plastic bags for ice. Hearing her 4-year-old <b>Crysthel</b> cry “<i>Ayo'ko, ayo'ko!</i>”, she ran up and saw Primo <b>kneeling</b> before Crysthel, whose pajamas and panty were off, his shorts down to his knees. She said she saw him forcing his penis into the child's vagina. She boxed him; he fled and was caught by relatives. The medical exam showed the <b>hymen intact</b> and no injury. The RTC convicted him of statutory rape and imposed <b>death</b>.",
r:"<b>Modified: attempted rape.</b>",
w:"For rape to be consummated, the penis must at least touch the <b>labia</b> of the pudendum: more than a “<b>mere strafing of the citadel</b>.” Here there was reasonable doubt. Given where she stood, the mother <b>could not have seen</b> any contact; Crysthel testified there was no penetration (she kept her legs closed); and the medical findings were negative. The overt acts commenced the crime but did not complete it, so it is attempted rape.",
d:"Consummated rape requires at least touching the labia; mere touching of the external genitalia without that is attempted rape.",
k:["She was making Milo","'Ayo'ko, ayo'ko!'","Primo was a helper bagging ice","Crysthel was 4 years old","See also People v. Agao (2022), the en banc update on 'inevitability' in child rape"]},

{id:"adiao",n:"U.S. v. Adiao",c:"38 Phil. 754 (1918)",t:"a6",g:"Theft",h:"Belt in desk",hc:"y",
f:"<b>Tomas Adiao</b>, a <b>customs inspector</b>, took a <b>leather belt</b> from the baggage of a Japanese passenger, <b>T. Murakami</b>, and hid it in his desk in the <b>Custom House</b>, where other employees found it. The lower courts convicted him of <b>frustrated</b> theft.",
r:"<b>Modified: consummated theft</b>, with the aggravating circumstance of taking advantage of his public position (3 months and 1 day of arresto mayor).",
w:"He performed all the acts of execution: the <b>taking</b> was complete when he took the belt and hid it in his desk. That he never got to <b>dispose</b> of it does not matter.",
d:"Theft is consummated once the thing is taken, even if the thief has no chance to dispose of it.",
k:["The belt belonged to T. Murakami","Hidden in his desk at the Custom House","Aggravated by abuse of public position"]},

{id:"valenzuela",n:"Valenzuela v. People",c:"525 SCRA 306 (2007, en banc)",t:"a6",g:"Theft",h:"Tide in a taxi",hc:"g",
f:"About 4:30 p.m. on <b>19 May 1994</b>, security guard <b>Lorenzo Lago</b> saw <b>Aristotel Valenzuela</b>, wearing an <b>RDU (Receiving Dispatching Unit) ID</b>, wheel a pushcart of <b>Tide</b> detergent out of the <b>Super Sale Club, SM North EDSA</b>, to the open parking area where <b>Jovy Calderon</b> waited. He returned for more, then hailed a <b>taxi</b>, and they loaded the boxes. Lago stopped the taxi before it left the lot and asked for a receipt. They ran; Lago fired a <b>warning shot</b> and both were caught. The goods were worth <b>₱12,090</b>.",
r:"<b>Consummated theft affirmed. There is no frustrated theft.</b>",
w:"Theft is produced the moment the offender gains <b>possession</b> of the thing with intent to gain (<i>apoderamiento</i>), even if he never had the chance to <b>dispose</b> of it. Being able to freely dispose of the property is not an element of theft (Art. 308), so theft can only be <b>attempted or consummated</b>. The Court abandoned <i>Diño</i> and <i>Flores</i> (the CA cases that had recognized frustrated theft).",
d:"Theft has no frustrated stage; unlawful taking is complete once the offender gains possession, even without the opportunity to dispose of the thing.",
k:["SM North EDSA Super Sale Club","Tide Ultramatic boxes, ₱12,090","Guard Lago fired a warning shot","Calderon waited in the parking lot"]},

{id:"lamahang",n:"People v. Lamahang",c:"61 Phil. 703 (1935)",t:"a6",g:"Robbery",h:"Iron bar, store wall",hc:"y",
f:"At dawn on <b>2 March 1935</b>, policeman <b>Jose Tomambing</b>, patrolling Delgado and C. R. Fuentes streets in <b>Iloilo</b>, caught <b>Aurelio Lamahang</b> making an opening with an <b>iron bar</b> in the wall of a store of cheap goods owned by <b>Tan Yu</b>, who was sleeping inside. Lamahang had broken one board and unfastened another. The CFI convicted him of attempted robbery.",
r:"<b>Modified: attempted trespass to dwelling</b> (by means of force).",
w:"An attempt requires overt acts with a <b>direct connection</b> to a <b>specific</b> crime. Breaking a wall shows an intent to <b>enter</b>, but what he meant to do inside (rob? rape? kill?) was uncertain. The attempt to commit an <b>indeterminate offense</b> is not a juridical fact. The only clear intent was to enter by force, and since the store was also a dwelling, the crime is attempted trespass to dwelling.",
d:"Overt acts must have an unavoidable connection to a definite crime; when the intended offense is indeterminate, the offender is liable only for what the acts clearly show.",
k:["Iron bar; one board broken, another unfastened","Tan Yu was asleep inside","Iloilo, at dawn","A habitual delinquent"]},

{id:"salvilla",n:"People v. Salvilla",c:"184 SCRA 671 (1990)",t:"a6",g:"Robbery",h:"Lumber-yard hostages",hc:"g",
f:"At noon on <b>12 April 1986</b>, Bienvenido Salvilla and the Canasares brothers, armed with <b>handguns and a hand grenade</b>, robbed the <b>New Iloilo Lumber Company</b> of owner <b>Severino Choco</b>: cash, watches, and jewelry. They held Severino, his daughters <b>Mary and Mimie</b>, and salesgirl <b>Rodita Hablero</b> hostage and demanded a ransom. After negotiations (including the OIC Mayor of Iloilo City), the military <b>assaulted the place</b>; Mary was seriously injured (her leg was later amputated). Salvilla argued that the robbery was only attempted because they never got away with the loot.",
r:"<b>Consummated robbery</b> with serious physical injuries and serious illegal detention; affirmed.",
w:"Robbery is consummated once the offender <b>gains possession</b> of the property, even without the chance to <b>dispose</b> of it or carry it away. Here the loot was taken and in their control.",
d:"Robbery is consummated upon unlawful taking (gaining possession); the opportunity to dispose of the loot is immaterial.",
k:["Hand grenade","New Iloilo Lumber Company","Mary Choco's leg was amputated"]},

{id:"borinaga",n:"People v. Borinaga",c:"55 Phil. 433 (1930)",t:"a6",g:"Murder / homicide",h:"Knife in chair",hc:"g",
f:"<b>Harry Mooney</b>, an American in <b>Calubian, Leyte</b>, refused to pay Juan Lawaan in full for a <b>fish corral</b> that was only about ⅔ finished; Basilio Borinaga was Lawaan's associate. That evening, Mooney sat on a chair in Perpetua Najarro's store with his back to the window. Borinaga, who had said “I will stab this Mooney, who is an American brute,” struck at him with a knife; the <b>knife lodged in the back of the chair</b> and Mooney fell, unhurt. About ten minutes later Borinaga came back to try again, but a <b>flashlight</b> was turned on him and he ran.",
r:"<b>Frustrated murder affirmed</b> (treachery). Villa-Real, J., with Johnson and Street, JJ., dissented: attempted murder.",
w:"The majority held that he performed all the acts of execution: a deadly blow aimed treacherously at the victim's back. It failed only because of the chair, a cause independent of his will. The dissent said that since no wound was inflicted, he had not performed all the acts of execution, so it was only an attempt. Modern doctrine leans toward the dissent: no mortal wound means an attempt.",
d:"Frustration requires that all acts of execution be performed; the majority found that here, but the split vote makes Borinaga the classic 'debate' case on the line between attempt and frustration.",
k:["The fish corral was ⅔ finished","'American brute'","Knife lodged in the back of the chair","Flashlight","Three justices dissented"],
x:"For exams, note both views: under current doctrine (e.g., People v. Pilones / Sy Pio reasoning), a non-mortal or no wound means the crime is attempted."},

{id:"sypio",n:"People v. Sy Pio",c:"94 Phil. 885 (1954)",t:"a6",g:"Murder / homicide",h:".45 in a Sta. Cruz store",hc:"y",
f:"Early on <b>3 Sept. 1949</b>, Sy Pio entered a store at <b>511 Misericordia, Sta. Cruz, Manila</b>, with a <b>.45 caliber pistol</b> and shot Jose Sy. <b>Tan Siong Kiap</b> asked, “What is the idea?” Sy Pio turned and shot him in the <b>right shoulder</b> (through to the back). Tan ran and <b>hid in a back room</b>; Sy Pio fired more shots and fled. Tan was hospitalized for 9 days and healed. (Sy Pio had also shot Ong Pian that morning; his grudges came from refused loans.)",
r:"<b>Modified: attempted murder</b> (not frustrated).",
w:"The <b>subjective phase</b> was not complete. Sy Pio saw Tan run and hide, so he knew he had <b>not performed all the acts of execution</b> needed to kill, and the wound was not fatal. Frustration requires that the offender believe he has done everything necessary to cause death.",
d:"A homicide or murder is frustrated only when the subjective phase is fully passed: the offender has performed all the acts of execution, believing they are enough to cause death.",
k:[".45 caliber pistol","511 Misericordia, Sta. Cruz","'What is the idea?'","Hit in the right shoulder; hid in a back room","9 days in the Chinese General Hospital"]},

{id:"ravelo",n:"People v. Ravelo",c:"202 SCRA 655 (1991)",t:"a6",g:"Murder / homicide",h:"CHDF 'NPA' interrogation",hc:"y",
f:"The accused, members of the <b>Civilian Home Defense Force (CHDF)</b> in Tandag, Surigao del Sur, seized <b>Reynaldo Cabrera Gaurano</b> on suspicion of being NPA, detained and maltreated him, and killed him (Crim. Case 1187). They also seized <b>Joey Lugatiman</b>, tied and interrogated him as a suspected NPA, mauled him, and told him he would be killed. He <b>escaped</b> and testified (Crim. Case 1194). The RTC convicted them of murder (Gaurano) and <b>frustrated murder</b> (Lugatiman).",
r:"<b>Murder affirmed</b> for Gaurano (reclusion perpetua). <b>For Lugatiman, modified to slight physical injuries</b> (arresto menor).",
w:"Frustrated (or even attempted) murder requires a proven <b>intent to kill</b> shown by overt acts. The <b>verbal threat</b> to kill and the injuries inflicted to <b>force a confession</b> did not prove intent to kill; his wounds were minor and there was no attack aimed at killing him. Without intent to kill, the crime falls to physical injuries.",
d:"Intent to kill must be proven by overt acts (weapon, wounds, manner of attack); a mere verbal threat does not make an assault frustrated murder.",
k:["CHDF","Tandag, Surigao del Sur","Lugatiman escaped and became the witness","Contrast: the four shots in Kalalo proved intent to kill"]},

{id:"kalalo",n:"People v. Kalalo",c:"59 Phil. 715 (1934)",t:"a6",g:"Murder / homicide",h:"Bolos over land",hc:"y",
f:"<b>Marcelo Kalalo</b> and <b>Isabela Holgado</b> had a land dispute in Barrio Calumpang, San Luis, <b>Batangas</b> (his two complaints had been dismissed). On <b>1 Oct. 1932</b>, Isabela's brother <b>Arcadio</b> had laborers plow the land. Marcelo, with his brothers Felipe and Juan and his brother-in-law Gregorio Ramos, all with <b>bolos</b>, ordered them to stop. Later, cousin <b>Marcelino Panaligan</b> arrived and ordered the laborers to resume. The Kalalo group attacked: Arcadio and Marcelino were <b>hacked to death</b>, and Marcelo took Marcelino's revolver and <b>fired four shots</b> at the fleeing <b>Hilarion Holgado</b>, missing him.",
r:"<b>Modified: homicide</b> for the two deaths (not murder) and <b>attempted homicide</b> for Hilarion.",
w:"Abuse of superior strength was not proven: the victims were also armed, so the sides were roughly <b>balanced</b>; without a qualifying circumstance it is homicide. Firing <b>four shots</b> at Hilarion showed <b>intent to kill</b>, but he was not hit, so the subjective phase was not complete: <b>attempted</b> homicide.",
d:"Intent to kill can be inferred from the means (repeated gunfire); a shooting that misses with intent to kill is an attempted homicide.",
k:["San Luis, Batangas, 1 Oct. 1932","Marcelino was an expert shot, yet no accused had a gunshot wound","Marcelo used the dead man's revolver","Four shots at the fleeing Hilarion"]},

// ───────── ART. 11 ─────────
{id:"boholst",n:"People v. Boholst-Caballero",c:"61 SCRA 180 (1974)",t:"a11",g:"Self-defense",h:"Choked on the road",hc:"r",
f:"Her husband, angry at finding <b>Cunigunda</b> out on the road in the middle of the night, knelt over her as she lay on her back and <b>choked her neck</b>. Nearly unconscious, she pulled the <b>knife tucked at the left side of his belt</b> and plunged it into his body (left lumbar region).",
r:"<b>Acquitted — complete self-defense.</b>",
w:"Choking is <b>unlawful aggression</b>. Grabbing the weapon within reach was <b>reasonable</b>, since the necessity of the means is judged by the imminent danger, not the harm done. Being out at night was at most a wrong imagined by the husband, not <b>sufficient provocation</b>. The location of the wound supported her story.",
d:"Self-defense protects rights (life, honor, property); reasonable necessity is judged by the imminent danger.",
k:["The knife was HIS, tucked in his belt","Choked while lying on her back"]},

{id:"alconga",n:"People v. Alconga",c:"78 Phil. 366 (1947)",t:"a11",g:"Self-defense",h:"Blackjack, two stages",hc:"g",
f:"In a <b>blackjack</b> game, Alconga stood behind the banker (the deceased) as a <b>spotter</b>, signaling to his partner. Later, as Alconga sat on a bench, the deceased struck at him with a <b>“pingahan”</b> (a carrying pole); Alconga crawled under the bench and fired his <b>revolver</b>. In the hand-to-hand fight that followed (dagger vs. bolo), the wounded deceased <b>ran away</b>; Alconga chased him and slashed his skull.",
r:"<b>Guilty of homicide.</b>",
w:"<b>First stage:</b> self-defense, since the deceased was the aggressor. <b>Second stage:</b> none, because the aggression <b>ceased</b> when the deceased ran away while Alconga was virtually unhurt. The pursuit and the fatal blow were retaliation, not defense.",
d:"When the aggressor flees, unlawful aggression ceases; what follows is retaliation.",
k:["Pingahan","Alconga was a spotter in blackjack","He fired from under the bench"]},

{id:"mack",n:"U.S. v. Mack",c:"8 Phil. 701 (1907)",t:"a11",g:"Self-defense",h:"Revolver vs. bolo",hc:"r",
f:"In <b>Tacloban, Leyte</b>, policemen ordered Olimpia to close her <b>tienda</b> and told soldiers <b>Anderson Mack</b> and Adams to go to their quarters. Policeman <b>Estanislao Indic</b>, cursing Mack for not obeying, broke away from a companion holding him back and advanced <b>brandishing his bolo</b>. At 3 to 6 feet, Mack rose, drew his <b>revolver</b>, and fired three shots. The trial court found only incomplete self-defense (he could have fled or aimed at the arms or legs).",
r:"<b>Acquitted — complete self-defense.</b>",
w:"With an armed attacker that close, it was doubtful there was even time to flee. In the heat of the moment, the defender <b>is not expected to aim carefully</b> at a less vital part. A revolver against a bolo at striking range is a <b>reasonable means</b> (rational equivalence, not material equality).",
d:"A person under attack need not calculate his blows; rational equivalence, not material equality, governs.",
k:["Tienda closing time","Policeman Indic was the aggressor","3–6 feet away; three shots"]},

{id:"sumicad",n:"People v. Sumicad",c:"56 Phil. 643 (1932)",t:"a11",g:"Self-defense",h:"Bully at chapel work",hc:"r",
f:"On 23 Feb. 1931, during <b>communal labor building a chapel</b> in Plaridel, Occidental Misamis, <b>Segundo Cubol</b>, a known <b>bully</b> who was larger and stronger, insulted <b>Julian Sumicad</b> and struck him with his fists. Sumicad retreated but was pursued and <b>cornered</b>. He drew his bolo and cut Cubol on the <b>right shoulder</b> as a warning; Cubol lunged to wrest the bolo away and was killed.",
r:"<b>Acquitted.</b> (Avanceña, C.J., and Villamor, J., dissented: only incomplete self-defense.)",
w:"He did not have to risk losing his only weapon to a violent, stronger man who could turn it on him. He had retreated and gave no sufficient provocation (he had only asked for what was owed). The means were reasonable under the circumstances.",
d:"The reasonableness of the means depends on the size, strength, and character of the aggressor, and on whether retreat is possible.",
k:["A chapel was being built","Cut on the right shoulder as a warning","Cubol tried to grab the bolo"]},

{id:"genosa",n:"People v. Genosa",c:"419 SCRA 537 (2004, en banc)",t:"a11",g:"Self-defense / BWS",h:"Battered wife, pipe",hc:"y",
f:"In <b>Isabel, Leyte</b>, on 15 Nov. 1995, after payday, Ben Genosa went to a <b>cockfight</b> and came home drunk. <b>Marivic</b>, <b>eight months pregnant</b>, struck him with a <b>metal pipe</b> after he went to the bedroom, then (per the evidence) shot him. The next day she boarded a bus to Ormoc with two of her children. The RTC convicted her of parricide with treachery and imposed death. On review, the Court sent the case back for expert testimony on <b>Battered Woman Syndrome</b>.",
r:"<b>Parricide, but the death penalty was set aside</b>: no treachery, plus two mitigating circumstances (psychological paralysis, akin to an illness that diminishes will-power; and passion and obfuscation).",
w:"BWS was recognized as a concept, but <b>not proven as complete self-defense</b>. The defense did not establish the full <b>cycle of violence at least twice</b> (tension-building, acute battering, tranquil/loving phase), and there was <b>no unlawful aggression at the moment of the killing</b> because Ben had stopped and gone to the bedroom.",
d:"BWS requires proof of the battering cycle occurring at least twice; unlawful aggression must exist when the defense is made. (RA 9262 § 26 now makes BWS a complete defense.)",
k:["Eight months pregnant","Metal pipe","Decided before RA 9262 took effect","Cockfight after payday"]},

{id:"luague",n:"People v. Luague",c:"62 Phil. 504 (1935)",t:"a11",g:"Defense of honor",h:"Kitchen, knife",hc:"r",
f:"On the morning of 18 Feb. 1935, in Lupuhan, Calatrava, Occidental Negros, <b>Natividad Luague</b> was home alone with her <b>three small children</b> while her husband was kilometers away <b>grinding corn</b>. Paulino Disuasido followed her into the <b>kitchen</b>, threatened her with a <b>knife</b>, embraced her, and tried to lie with her. She mortally wounded him. The prosecution's theory: the jealous husband and wife conspired.",
r:"<b>Both acquitted.</b>",
w:"Attempted rape is <b>unlawful aggression against honor</b>, a right as precious as life; a woman may kill if that is her only means of protection. The conspiracy theory collapsed on its own facts.",
d:"A woman's honor is a right that may be defended even with lethal force when there is no other means.",
k:["Husband was grinding corn far away","In the kitchen","Three small children at home"]},

{id:"delacruz",n:"People v. De la Cruz",c:"61 Phil. 344 (1935)",t:"a11",g:"Defense of honor",h:"Grabbed on dark path",hc:"r",
f:"Walking home after dark on a narrow path, with her companions far ahead, she was grabbed <b>from behind</b> by Francisco Rivera, who seized her breasts, kissed her, touched her private parts, and began to throw her down. She took a <b>knife from her pocket</b> and stabbed him.",
r:"<b>Acquitted — defense of honor.</b>",
w:"Darkness, isolation, and his strength meant she had <b>no other means</b> of defending her honor; the knife was a reasonable means.",
d:"Defense of honor is complete when the place and circumstances leave the woman no other means.",
k:["Dark, narrow path","Pocket knife","Grabbed from behind"],
x:"Some summaries say they were “already having sexual relations” and call it a mistake of fact. That is not how Reyes or the decision frame it; rely on the facts above."},

{id:"jaurigue",n:"People v. Jaurigue",c:"76 Phil. 174 (1946)",t:"a11",g:"Defense of honor",h:"Fan knife in chapel",hc:"y",
f:"<b>Avelina Jaurigue</b> had rejected <b>Amado Capiña</b>'s courtship; he once embraced and kissed her (she punched and kicked him), so she began carrying a <b>fan knife</b>. A week later, in a <b>lighted chapel</b> with people present, including <b>her father and the barrio lieutenant</b>, he sat beside her and put his hand on her <b>upper right thigh</b>. She stabbed him at the base of the left side of his neck.",
r:"<b>Homicide, mitigated</b> (incomplete self-defense, plus voluntary surrender and other mitigating circumstances).",
w:"There was <b>unlawful aggression</b> against her honor, but the <b>means were excessive</b>: in a lighted chapel full of people, including her father, there was no chance of rape. Slapping or punching him would have been justified.",
d:"Defense of honor fails as a complete defense when the means are excessive for the actual danger (place and occasion count).",
k:["Fan knife","Hand on the upper right thigh","Her father and the barrio lieutenant were present","Lighted chapel"]},

{id:"apolinar",n:"People v. Apolinar",c:"C.A., 38 O.G. 2870",t:"a11",g:"Defense of property",h:"Shot the palay thief",hc:"g",
f:"Armed with a <b>shotgun</b> and looking over his land at night, Apolinar (“Atong”) saw a man carrying a bundle he believed was his stolen <b>palay</b>. He shouted “stop,” fired in the air, then fired at the fleeing man, killing him.",
r:"<b>Not justified (homicide).</b>",
w:"Defense of property justifies force only when coupled with an <b>attack on the person</b> defending it. The fleeing thief made <b>no unlawful aggression</b> against him. Had the thief swung a bolo at him, the shooting would have been justified.",
d:"Defense of property requires an attack on the person guarding it (under the old rule); property alone does not justify killing.",
k:["Shotgun","He fired in the air first","The thief was fleeing with the palay"]},

{id:"bumanglag",n:"U.S. v. Bumanglag",c:"14 Phil. 644 (1909)",t:"a11",g:"Defense of property",h:"Sheathed bolo",hc:"g",
f:"In January 1909, in San Nicolas, Ilocos Norte, 40 bundles of Bumanglag's palay went missing from his granary and turned up in a <b>sugarcane field</b> 100 m away. That night he lay in wait with others. Guillermo Ribis came for the palay and was killed. They said they used only <b>sticks</b> after Ribis drew his bolo, but his <b>bolo was found still sheathed</b> and the wounds were from cutting and stabbing weapons.",
r:"<b>Homicide</b> (mitigated); only Bundoc appealed. Moreland and Carson, JJ., dissented to acquit.",
w:"There was <b>no unlawful aggression</b>. The <b>sheathed bolo</b> and the cutting wounds contradicted their story, and lying in wait shows they meant to punish, not defend.",
d:"Physical evidence (a sheathed weapon, the nature of the wounds) can destroy a claim of self-defense or defense of property.",
k:["40 bundles of palay hidden in sugarcane","The bolo was still in its sheath","Sticks vs. cutting wounds"]},

{id:"narvaez",n:"People v. Narvaez",c:"121 SCRA 389 (1983)",t:"a11",g:"Defense of property",h:"Chiselled walls",hc:"y",
f:"On 22 Aug. 1968, in Maitum, South Cotabato, in a land dispute with Fleischer &amp; Co., Fleischer and Flaviano Rubia (Narvaez's <b>compadre</b>) had laborers fence the land, <b>chiselling the walls</b> of his house and rice mill and cutting off his access to the highway. Asked to stop, Fleischer said, “No, gadamit, proceed, go ahead.” Narvaez fired his <b>shotgun from the window</b>, killing both, then surrendered with the gun.",
r:"<b>Murder reduced to homicide</b> (incomplete defense of property as a privileged mitigating circumstance, plus voluntary surrender and passion/obfuscation); he was released after about 14 years in detention.",
w:"There was unlawful aggression against his <b>property rights</b> (Civil Code Art. 429), and he gave no provocation, but <b>shooting to kill was disproportionate</b> to an attack on property, so the second requisite failed.",
d:"An attack on property rights can be unlawful aggression, but killing to defend property is generally unreasonable; the defense is incomplete.",
k:["'No, gadamit, proceed, go ahead'","Chiselled walls of his house and rice mill","Shotgun from the window","Rubia was his compadre"]},

{id:"chuahiong",n:"People v. Chua Hiong",c:"C.A., 51 O.G. 1932 (1954)",t:"a11",g:"Defense of reputation",h:"Libel answered by libel",hc:"r",
f:"Chua Hiong published a reply to attacks by his <b>nephew</b>, Cesario T. Gocheco, saying the nephew seemed “obsessed with a persecution mania” and was out to besmirch his name and harass his family. He was convicted of libel below.",
r:"<b>Acquitted.</b>",
w:"A person who is libeled may <b>hit back with another libel</b>, which is justified if it is <b>adequate</b> (a reasonable reply). Unlike a physical attack, the sting of defamation persists, so the defamed person may use all necessary means to shake it off.",
d:"Self-defense extends to reputation: an adequate libelous reply to a libel is justified.",
k:["The libelous critic was his nephew","'Persecution mania'"]},

{id:"pelayo",n:"People v. Pelayo, Jr.",c:"64 O.G. 1991",t:"a11",g:"Defense of reputation",h:"Accused the governor",hc:"g",
f:"Pantaleon Pelayo, Jr. told a lawyer that a Chinese gambling operator, Lim Peng, had said then-Governor <b>Alejandro Almendras</b> was taking monthly protection money. The next day he made a similar speech before the <b>city council</b>. He claimed the governor had defamed him first.",
r:"<b>Convicted of light oral defamation.</b>",
w:"Self-defense of reputation covers <b>explaining one's side</b> and repairing the damage. Hitting back with an <b>unrelated</b> or equally scurrilous accusation is an independent act of defamation that he must answer for. This is the limit of <i>Chua Hiong</i>.",
d:"A defamatory reply on a different matter is not self-defense; the reply must be limited to repairing the damage.",
k:["Governor Almendras","Protection money from gambling","Speech before the city council"]},

{id:"esmedia",n:"U.S. v. Esmedia",c:"17 Phil. 260 (1910)",t:"a11",g:"Defense of relative",h:"Father in the mud",hc:"y",
f:"In a rice-land dispute between related families, Gregorio Esmedia fought his brother-in-law <b>Santiago Abando</b>. Gregorio's sons, <b>Ponciano and Mena Esmedia</b>, arrived as the fight ended and saw their father <b>lying in the mud and water</b>, fatally wounded, with Santiago standing over him. They killed Santiago. They also killed Santiago's father, Ciriaco, who arrived later.",
r:"<b>Justified as to Santiago; homicide as to Ciriaco.</b>",
w:"For Santiago: they <b>honestly believed</b> he would strike their father again; unlawful aggression may be judged by the honest belief of the one making the defense. For Ciriaco: he made <b>no aggression</b> at all.",
d:"In defense of relatives, unlawful aggression may rest on the defender's honest, reasonable belief.",
k:["Father lying in mud and water","Two victims, two different results"]},

{id:"normahernandez",n:"People v. Norma Hernandez",c:"C.A., 55 O.G. 8465 (1959)",t:"a11",g:"Avoidance of greater evil",h:"Runaway bride",hc:"r",
f:"Norma Hernandez was about to marry Vivencio Lascano, who had already prepared the wedding. She confessed she had never loved him and had been pressured by relatives, and she <b>eloped with the man she loved</b>. She was charged with <b>serious slander by deed</b>.",
r:"<b>Acquitted.</b>",
w:"Avoiding a <b>loveless marriage</b> is avoiding a greater evil. “Damage to another” in Art. 11(4) covers injury to <b>honor</b>. There was also no malice: she was exercising her right to withhold consent to marriage.",
d:"Art. 11(4) covers damage to honor; escaping a loveless marriage is avoiding a greater evil.",
k:["Fiancé Vivencio Lascano","Charged with slander by deed"]},

{id:"dudley",n:"Regina v. Dudley and Stephens",c:"14 QBD 273 (1884)",t:"a11",g:"Avoidance of greater evil",h:"Cabin boy eaten",hc:"g",
f:"The yacht <b>Mignonette</b> sank; four men were left in a dinghy. After about 20 days adrift, Dudley killed the weakened 17-year-old <b>cabin boy, Richard Parker</b> (Stephens assented; Brooks refused), and they fed on his body. A German barque rescued them four days later.",
r:"<b>Murder.</b> The death sentence was commuted to six months.",
w:"Necessity does not justify killing an innocent person to save one's own life. In Philippine terms: a life for a life is not avoiding a <b>greater</b> evil, so Art. 11(4) fails; and Art. 12(5)/(6) requires compulsion by another person.",
d:"Necessity is no defense to killing an innocent.",
k:["Mignonette","Richard Parker, 17","Brooks refused to take part","Rescued four days later"]},

{id:"ty",n:"Ty v. People",c:"439 SCRA 220 (2004)",t:"a11",g:"Avoidance of greater evil",h:"Hospital bill checks",hc:"g",
f:"Vicky C. Ty's mother and sister ran up <b>₱1,075,592.95</b> at <b>Manila Doctors Hospital</b>. She issued <b>seven postdated ₱30,000 checks</b> that bounced (BP 22). She claimed the hospital treated her mother inhumanely and would not let her leave until the bill was paid.",
r:"<b>Convicted</b> (BP 22).",
w:"<b>Uncontrollable fear</b> fails: the fear was speculative, not real and imminent. <b>State of necessity</b> fails: there was no actual evil, and less harmful means existed. The supposed danger was also brought about by her own failure to pay, and her lawyer had warned her not to issue the checks.",
d:"The evil avoided must actually exist and must not be caused by the actor; there must be no other practical and less harmful means.",
k:["Seven ₱30,000 checks","Manila Doctors Hospital","Her lawyer advised against issuing the checks"]},

{id:"delima",n:"People v. Delima",c:"46 Phil. 738 (1922)",t:"a11",g:"Fulfillment of duty",h:"Bamboo lance escapee",hc:"r",
f:"<b>Lorenzo Napilon</b> escaped from jail. Policeman <b>Felipe Delima</b> found him in Jorge Alegria's house armed with a <b>pointed bamboo lance</b> and ordered him to surrender; Napilon thrust at him. Delima dodged and fired (and missed); Napilon fled, still armed. Delima pursued and fired again, killing him. The CFI convicted him of homicide.",
r:"<b>Acquitted — fulfillment of duty.</b>",
w:"The escapee was bound to surrender and resisted with a weapon, which forced the officer to use an extreme means. This is not self-defense, since the aggression had ceased when Napilon fled, but the killing was the <b>necessary consequence of the due performance of duty</b> (capturing an armed, resisting fugitive).",
d:"Killing an armed, resisting escapee in the course of recapture is justified as fulfillment of duty, even when self-defense would fail.",
k:["Pointed bamboo lance","Found in Jorge Alegria's house","Missed the first shot"]},

{id:"belbes",n:"People v. Belbes",c:"334 SCRA 161 (2000)",t:"a11",g:"Fulfillment of duty",h:"Armalite at the prom",hc:"y",
f:"Patrolman <b>Domingo Belbes</b> and P/Cpl. Jose Pabon were keeping order at a <b>Junior-Senior Prom</b> at Pili Barangay High School (Albay). Responding to a report of trouble, Belbes fired an <b>armalite</b> at the drunk Fernando Bataller, killing him; he claimed Bataller had thrust a knife at Pabon and at him. A witness said about <b>six seconds</b> passed between Belbes leaving his seat and the burst of gunfire.",
r:"<b>Homicide</b> (not murder), with the privileged mitigating circumstance of <b>incomplete fulfillment of duty</b>.",
w:"Self-defense was unproven. He was <b>on duty</b> (first requisite), but firing without warning was not the <b>necessary consequence</b> of keeping order at a prom (second requisite absent), so the justification was incomplete.",
d:"Fulfillment of duty requires that the injury be the necessary consequence of performing the duty; excess makes it incomplete (privileged mitigating).",
k:["Junior-Senior Prom","Armalite","About six seconds"]},

{id:"beronilla",n:"People v. Beronilla",c:"96 Phil. 566 (1955)",t:"a11",g:"Obedience to superior's order",h:"12 bolomen jury",hc:"r",
f:"<b>Manuel Beronilla</b>, appointed <b>Military Mayor of La Paz, Abra</b> (1944) by Lt. Col. Arnold, was authorized to try collaborators before a jury of <b>12 “bolomen.”</b> Wartime mayor <b>Arsenio Borjal</b> was tried for 19 days and convicted; when consulted, Beronilla's superiors replied that the disposition was up to La Paz. Borjal was executed the night of <b>18 April 1945</b>. A radiogram from Col. Volckmann questioning the trial was never shown to have reached Beronilla. The CFI convicted him of murder.",
r:"<b>Acquitted.</b>",
w:"Military subordinates who obey superior orders in <b>good faith</b>, <b>unaware of their illegality</b> and <b>without negligence</b>, act without criminal intent (<i>actus non facit reum nisi mens sit rea</i>).",
d:"Good-faith obedience to an order not known to be illegal negates criminal intent.",
k:["12 bolomen as the jury","Volckmann radiogram","Executed on 18 April 1945","Trial lasted 19 days"],
x:"Some summaries say the case involved executing “guerrilla sympathizers without trial.” That is wrong: Borjal was tried as a collaborator (before the bolomen jury)."}
];
// Extra case, not on the syllabus list
window.CASES.push({id:"agao",n:"People v. Agao (extra; not on the syllabus)",c:"(2022, en banc)",t:"a6",g:"Rape",h:"Inevitability rule",hc:"g",
f:"A stepfather repeatedly tried to rape his 10-year-old stepdaughter, AAA; he could not fully penetrate because she fought back, but she indicated (using a doll) that his penis had touched her vulval cleft. A second incident happened when she was 13.",
r:"<b>One count of statutory rape and one of simple rape</b> (reclusion perpetua each).",
w:"The Court en banc refined <i>Campuhan</i>: carnal knowledge includes the penis touching the labia; in rape of a child, consummation may also be found where the evidence shows the entry was <b>inevitable</b> but for the immaturity of the child's organ. Leonen, J. (dissenting in part): “rape is rape,” so the attempt/consummation lines are artificial.",
d:"Refines Campuhan; still, no frustrated rape.",
k:["Not on the syllabus; the 2022 refinement of Campuhan"]});
